Z||000013||Old Lar||01-29-2001||11:02 PM||||Hy I've been to mainland China and anything that moves can be on your plate.  Starvation during the cultral revolution does wonders to open up your taste buds.<P>While there I had turtle, frog, snake, eel, snails, squid, duck, chicken, prawns, octopus, pidgeon, fish(?), "green spinach like stuff".  When in Rome...  Mostly the food was quite good and yes dog and cat could be purchased, but I did not knowenly eat either.  Dog and cat are pricey.<P>At the restaurant, there were tanks of fresh food swimming, and you get to pick what you want.  At one place I ask my host, pointing to the tank and asked "Is that a moray eel?" and was warned not to point or it will be on your plate.<P>The only thing I didn't care for was the frog, rather slimy, or it was the way they cooked it.  The snake that was served was boney (as was the eel) and more work than I wanted to get the meat.<P>Business trips, 1998, 1999 and 2000.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Black88GT||01-29-2001||11:15 PM||||Like Old Lar said, when you have billions of starving people, anything that can be eaten will be eaten (aside from humans of course).<P>Like Darklord said, eating cow meat is considered just as disturbing in other cultures, namely the middle east.  I am pretty sure in Jewish culture that your not supposed to eat cow meat, it might be just Jerusalem, not sure.  Anyhoo, everyone knows that if its a question of survival, we will do anything to survive....<P>p.s. that cartoon had me LMAO!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
